From 800fdf1c63fc1fb3d195d6372fd1f89c88f54732 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 00:39:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] dce: replace refcount worklist with mark-and-sweep,
 multi-def-safe

The audited mark-and-sweep variant is adopted - the use-count worklist
in the tree cannot collect dead phi CYCLES (an unused IV is
%p = phi[0,%a]; %a = add %p,1: both keep use_count 1 forever; the
phi-self-reference exclusion only patches the trivial phi V:[0,V]
shape). Leftover dead phis lower to copies that burned registers and
historically corrupted inline-asm output slots (kernel crash in
init_scattered_cpuid_features).

TWO BUGS IN THE PROPOSED MARK-AND-SWEEP FIXED BEFORE ADOPTION:

1. MULTI-DEF UNSAFETY (would be a new miscompile): this pass also runs
   AFTER eliminate_phis (post-phi cleanup in the driver), where the IR
   is NOT SSA - phi lowering emits Copy{dest:%d} in EVERY predecessor.
   A single-slot def map keeps only the last-seen site, marks only that
   copy live, and sweeps the others: %d is garbage on every other
   inbound path. def_loc now diverts multi-def ids to an overflow table
   whose sites are marked live TOGETHER (and all swept when dead).
   Tests: test_multi_def_post_phi_copies_all_kept (unsafe direction),
   test_multi_def_dead_copies_all_removed (conservative direction).

2. SEEDING-ORDER UNSAFETY: the proposal seeded terminator uses while
   scanning defs block-by-block. Block indices are not dominance-ordered
   after CFG transforms, so a terminator use of a value defined in a
   HIGHER-indexed block hit def_loc while it still read NO_DEF - the
   live def would be swept. Terminator seeding is now a second pass
   after def_loc is complete. Test: test_terminator_use_of_later_block_def.

Further hardening beyond the proposal:
- PgoCounterInc added to has_side_effects (was only saved by the
  dest-less fallback; the predicate is now truthful).
- sweep_block debug_assert on flag/instruction length (fail fast in
  debug, refuse-to-sweep in release).
- Honest comments: Rust Vec<bool> is NOT bit-packed (that is C++
  vector<bool>) - measured size_of::<bool>()==1; the audit's u8-vs-bool
  performance claim was wrong and is documented as such.
- OnceLock-cached CCC_DEBUG_DCE, with_capacity worklist,
  saturating_add def_len, ~80 LOC dead eliminate_dead_code_simple
  removed, retain-based sweep, 14 unit tests (was 4).

PROOF: 673 lib tests (+10), 165/165 x86 regression, ARM differential
95/30 (up from 94 - a dead-IV cycle was pinning a register there too),
50/50 correctness, 3/3 intrinsics, 300/300 differential fuzz.
PERF: nbody kernel 0.847s -> 0.665s (-21%) on the same machine within
one session - the collected IV cycles were burning registers in the
FP loop; output bit-identical to gcc. sieve/hashloop/matmul unchanged
within noise.
